Since the numbers are sorted, a binary search can keep looking to the right whenever it sees `x`. The current match is saved before the search continues, which gives the rightmost matching position.

Dry run with `[1, 2, 2, 2, 5]` and `x = 2`: a match is found, then the search range moves right until no later `2` remains.

```java
class Solution {
    public int upperBound(int[] nums, int x) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = nums.length - 1;
        int answer = -1;

        while (left <= right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (nums[middle] == x) {
                answer = middle;
                left = middle + 1;
            } else if (nums[middle] < x) {
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle - 1;
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

The input stays unchanged. If `x` is smaller than every value or larger than every value, the normal search rules still return the correct result.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

Starting at the right side makes the first match the answer. This avoids checking the rest of the array once `x` is found.

The array can be empty, so the loop simply never runs in that case.

