A direct scan is enough here. Each time the value equals `x`, I store its index, so the final stored index is the last occurrence. If no value matches, the answer stays `-1`.

For `[1, 2, 2, 4]` and `x = 2`, the saved index changes from `1` to `2`.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    int upperBound(vector<int> &nums, int x){
        int answer = -1;

        for (int i = 0; i < static_cast<int>(nums.size()); i++) {
            if (nums[i] == x) {
                answer = i;
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
};
```

This does not change the input array. An empty array and a value that is not present both return `-1`.

Time complexity: `O(n)`  
Space complexity: `O(1)`

This version uses a recursive binary search with a half-open range. When the middle value is less than or equal to `x`, the useful part is on the right. If the middle value equals `x`, it is kept as a possible answer first.

The recursion stops when the search range becomes empty, so a missing value is handled without a separate special case.

```cpp
class Solution{
    int findLast(vector<int> &nums, int left, int right, int x) {
        if (left >= right) {
            return -1;
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (nums[middle] > x) {
            return findLast(nums, left, middle, x);
        }

        int rightAnswer = findLast(nums, middle + 1, right, x);
        if (rightAnswer != -1) {
            return rightAnswer;
        }

        return nums[middle] == x ? middle : -1;
    }

public:
    int upperBound(vector<int> &nums, int x){
        return findLast(nums, 0, static_cast<int>(nums.size()), x);
    }
};
```

The array is not changed. The recursive calls use stack space, unlike an iterative binary search.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`  
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the recursion stack

